course: dictionary
lesson: 11591
title: Anymore or any more?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Anymore means "no longer", as in "not any longer". Something used to happen, but it has stopped. «I don't play football anymore.» The clue is that the sentence is negative, with not or no before anymore.
explain: Anymore almost always comes at the end of a negative sentence or question. «The dog doesn't bark anymore.» If there is no not or no in the sentence, anymore is probably the wrong word.
explain: Any more, written as two words, means an extra amount. «Would you like any more juice?» means "some additional juice". Test it by putting "extra" in the sentence. If "extra" fits, use any more, with two words.
fact: Anymore means now, as before but no longer.
fact: «Anymore» is used with not or no: «I don't live there anymore.»
fact: «Anymore» is an adverb, a word that tells more about a verb, such as when or how something happens.
fact: Some American speakers use anymore to mean nowadays, but this is less common.
fact: «Any more» (two words) means an additional amount: «Do you want any more toast?»
exercise: «She doesn't visit her grandma anymore.» What does anymore mean here? | Very much | Every day from now on | *No longer, she has stopped visiting | Again and again
exercise: Which sentence uses anymore correctly? | «Anymore I play with dolls today.» | «I play with dolls anymore.» | *«I don't play with dolls anymore.» | «I anymore play with dolls.»
exercise: «Would you like any more pie?» What does any more mean? | *Some extra pie | Pie that is no longer fresh | No pie at all | Pie for tomorrow
test: «Ben doesn't wear his old trainers anymore.» Why is anymore right here? | *It means no longer, and it follows a negative with not | It means very old | It means happy | It means additional, so any more is needed
test: Mum asks, «Do you want ___ biscuits?» Which words fit the gap? | at all | *any more | anywhere | anymore
exam: «The old bus stop isn't there anymore.» What does this sentence mean? | The bus stop is still there, and busier than ever | The bus stop is closed for the day | The bus stop has moved into town | *The bus stop is no longer there
